#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dc989a8-2c75-5f1c-9659-077b9b5ceeed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349599} {:answer/id #uuid "90451e36-0f3c-542c-9624-ec09beef5517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349742} {:answer/id #uuid "96633e09-e5d2-5a83-a534-6acb8fbe9b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349795} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d311c7-63bd-56a8-90ae-daf55b933be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350028} {:answer/id #uuid "eec3251e-786c-5a1a-9eba-fcc26b7bd286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350534}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349795} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e5ea7c-8d82-5535-96fc-e39fd184f9e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d35cfdf-da63-5e6d-990c-a2e5c83f1725" :question/math-academy-id "q-8311" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=0$ given that $y(-1)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=-4x+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(-1)=4$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-4\\cdot (-1)+C \\\\ 4 & =4+C \\\\ 0 & =C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-4x\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0824f56f-20f9-5a23-a152-8fec76baadc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}+3}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351637} {:answer/id #uuid "c63c9edf-13ba-5348-a784-f544f8f7798a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-3{v}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353287} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bb8d1f-0d25-549e-bf56-0e3e7da24b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}-5v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353298} {:answer/id #uuid "ea09a4b8-ac8e-5fa4-97cc-6fd6b8f5c6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353605} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dfbe1c-3320-559f-b09b-1366b8a1de9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353752} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a68c90-9059-546d-a30a-825cc44d6aa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ed802b3-b056-5f83-8a03-3932e7ced220" :question/math-academy-id "q-8747" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{vx}{x}\\right)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31321e37-1d8f-571b-93be-de318d95581f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+{e}^{Kx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352023} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5509a4-9f52-54c9-a17c-80239e35bbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352393} {:answer/id #uuid "91cfd736-1c68-561f-a5b9-ea0cbbe64c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K(2+{e}^{3x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352829} {:answer/id #uuid "a325eecd-f037-58fb-8914-e5fc2b454b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{Kx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352960} {:answer/id #uuid "dff7fb2b-7bf1-5f3a-9233-5a286fcbeca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352960} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3b6679e-da5e-570b-8272-44a851986baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b284307a-7bac-5101-a812-75d87d197409" :question/math-academy-id "q-8845" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)+1)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{vx}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{vx}{x}\\right)+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo evaluate the integral on the left-hand side, we use integration by substitution with\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{ln}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{u}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡v| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡v| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{C}|x|) \\\\ |\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =|x|{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x} & =\\pm {e}^{C}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x} & =Kx \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & ={e}^{Kx} \\\\ y & =x{e}^{Kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced the new constant of integration:\n\n$$\nK=\\pm {e}^{C}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c733e-9f50-59f1-a6c2-d2c461d141a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355126} {:answer/id #uuid "567ab48d-395e-5349-9130-02d9670bd228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355436} {:answer/id #uuid "6402d895-e12f-57c4-ad2c-f9930e976cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355555} {:answer/id #uuid "6404e39a-62ed-5cc9-8470-5899b49795e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355556} {:answer/id #uuid "f94f8b4b-d656-5e4d-a481-417dad03fa18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355555} :answer-field/id #uuid "1299ce9b-cffd-57b7-832d-bc7388915ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc83a0da-147c-536d-ae44-6851708bb02f" :question/math-academy-id "q-90597" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37195fe4-1e69-5e29-9f64-88c2decbd220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355158} {:answer/id #uuid "d7944865-4cd9-5653-8711-296c10bff723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356552} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb55cc2-621e-5cbe-9cd9-9ff6c96826e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356599} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb8b2a1-b08b-5a4b-bd39-b2a6e5fddc52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356600} {:answer/id #uuid "e91d297a-9ec5-5912-aa6d-9d3765e8b68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356552} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02fb817-012c-5fd8-afd9-ec9e45613dab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "647415db-3b65-5b2e-8b3d-f70381af49e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-90601" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{vx}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{vx}{x}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433c74fb-43f7-5727-9983-010f8c211f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{4}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355264} {:answer/id #uuid "55a4be35-898b-5439-adca-7b36f1ff4970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{C+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355425} {:answer/id #uuid "a96d1ec6-5cb0-56d5-9973-2123a3ad4d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356163} {:answer/id #uuid "cc536521-0c37-5947-8b9c-28f5c636543e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356447} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb3678b-a603-59dc-9e12-878859c35e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356163} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd025383-e1bb-5f14-a031-96c47932a3f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51ab68ae-6386-58f8-941d-fb2c589605a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-90688" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{({v}^{2}+1){x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (v+\\frac{1}{v})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({v}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) & =C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})} & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324d9632-b193-587f-bfb5-d3128eedb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355105} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea6a89a-49ac-5d60-8c9f-b573869dd1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355366} {:answer/id #uuid "a748de52-875c-59b6-9078-bb8480995243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356146} {:answer/id #uuid "b93929bd-c6ff-5cde-a0dc-c9ed5ef022e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356286} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a14662-7280-54dd-9109-57e225b61eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356286} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa93ac0-f184-5991-a79d-78c9b1ef6575" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b4ed6f3-beb2-5be5-83cb-e8d41e7b8f3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90691" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}(1+{v}^{3})}{{v}^{2}{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1+{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}}+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0230625f-08fa-547b-9a26-94bd2ec91119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354675} {:answer/id #uuid "30fd4d79-be88-5dd8-9bab-ff864e0801bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355093} {:answer/id #uuid "351f175f-a66a-537e-a899-d5e28a2bf51f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355136} {:answer/id #uuid "9738c27c-b600-5b8d-bf29-19ef94ea0e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355991} {:answer/id #uuid "ab45bf3d-adde-5478-b43f-fad1e155bfef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355991} :answer-field/id #uuid "008bd79e-26b4-5414-8726-6a649de49016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed854c8a-5a47-5533-b48c-04deb7b81625" :question/math-academy-id "q-90692" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+2(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+2v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+2v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}|x|) \\\\ |v+1| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}|x| \\\\ v+1 & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}x \\\\ v+1 & =Cx \\\\ v & =Cx-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =Cx-1 \\\\ y & =C{x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db1daca-94f7-5fc9-a2ae-4b63f7bd12e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355215} {:answer/id #uuid "4053804e-1ea5-55b1-bd74-16ff29c5bfb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355239} {:answer/id #uuid "538b21c4-4f74-52e0-9fdc-8f28be2e423d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355407} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa45948-76de-50a2-ab36-7a90839c9027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355890} {:answer/id #uuid "a42f0e08-13ac-59b0-9899-3b551131684e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355407} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a371f5e-aae2-5ed7-90ed-8749fc23270a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73b06f86-eb7f-5333-916f-45bb6952f791" :question/math-academy-id "q-90693" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a1175c-b88d-5694-b7e7-526c951376dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-8$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355393} {:answer/id #uuid "55358244-95ad-5be8-8c31-191c2b6cd49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-6$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355419} {:answer/id #uuid "718ab0aa-9712-5e6c-89eb-85c512e5390a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355665} {:answer/id #uuid "977ed467-68e0-5fb9-be92-4ae0952c10af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ or $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355998} {:answer/id #uuid "9a029f58-a010-5459-bc3b-36a37b1e50ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ or $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356031} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ac4aaa-4b33-5941-8e97-7884551f59ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4cbc6a7d-b508-53fb-a60e-7e0a5e087bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-90705" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-24y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22963fd1-3a86-53e1-9d50-58a544250ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354958} {:answer/id #uuid "33af2c38-5c2e-5b47-a6b9-8aa7d86068ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-7$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355120} {:answer/id #uuid "99583a56-5f05-513f-92ba-5c5f2ac20db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ or $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356026} {:answer/id #uuid "b89efec5-ee5d-5db2-a5c2-b3391132dd1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356282} {:answer/id #uuid "e836b5f3-3182-5c7c-851a-e1d3da55c802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-14$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b039a1-2dd5-5566-951a-e168f701f927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2680a0da-7f34-50eb-834d-2854fcbc4646" :question/math-academy-id "q-90709" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-14y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-7$ or $\\lambda =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c53b0e-6d9a-5a83-bf41-08b1929f0bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354680} {:answer/id #uuid "23c04ba8-e203-5d92-877b-743ffcdc9045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354965} {:answer/id #uuid "3eea3753-cfa7-5409-b31b-24702ddd6273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355226} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbf221d-7c59-5836-9798-f17cb8bd6ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356442} {:answer/id #uuid "cd476d30-432c-55a6-af5a-4916bdf18b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355226} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f890261-7007-5f0d-a351-2babd22ea3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53fbca9b-a9e4-5e0a-8181-574662143d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-90719" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+15y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =3$ or $\\lambda =5.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1ea764-4dca-5820-9034-d4b4f70f730b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355221} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae4ce25-9e97-5331-8ed8-96b2d9024430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355466} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba0b662-5fc6-5644-94c1-0e8b93d1e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355900} {:answer/id #uuid "aff3c5d4-9583-5b46-a41e-d92a6fb9fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356214} {:answer/id #uuid "d0936d80-a410-5f00-b078-134eaae76756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355900} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf81e32-4f57-597a-af29-291088b46e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb0ac50b-6a2d-5d53-997b-cb65e0ad2bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-90720" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =1$ or $\\lambda =6.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f538e8-ffd5-580a-b787-3529e176d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354683} {:answer/id #uuid "7577a907-a24b-5a1a-9252-dbbbb82ae05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355696} {:answer/id #uuid "90cf1316-9d50-5a6d-896f-516e72559dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-14x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355942} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ddbcdd-67e2-59df-87ae-7afbcd997f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-7x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356128} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bcbb48-4d50-5e65-a98a-b2d2871a3b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354683} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c598865-38e3-50dc-b0e0-62772d08c731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5c18e84-2e36-57eb-8c9a-1a101f9f859c" :question/math-academy-id "q-90722" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-11\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+28y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-11\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+28{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f617da-c83d-552a-846a-2c0fa0590093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354872} {:answer/id #uuid "38b5fa43-aac8-50ca-b673-60402ca722b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2(x+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355174} {:answer/id #uuid "4f225f02-b37a-5b2e-b567-ee8cccaec207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355371} {:answer/id #uuid "65aa3ded-349b-5fda-b9d6-b30d41271208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355570} {:answer/id #uuid "6eaf1ced-1abf-527a-9fa8-e99808b061e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355643} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6cf717-d71d-5e47-b39a-f6162f536688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6ebbe36-257b-53a0-8f7f-d2ce63fbc0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-90727" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8134cb-757e-5d42-9e52-e1be730cd855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6x}+2{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354790} {:answer/id #uuid "3529b0ab-4f12-594c-a2f7-c68b64eaf2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}-8{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355139} {:answer/id #uuid "7d31d0c1-0468-5e56-877c-19e818ac36c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355768} {:answer/id #uuid "d13b07b9-d726-574c-9d7e-e27a8da7875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356495} {:answer/id #uuid "df457980-9f43-5760-a71d-325601bca0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356618} :answer-field/id #uuid "019cf9ab-0c62-56d0-bba2-30385b4bf346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "937412e4-d54f-5aaa-9272-a7d19f395a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-90729" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+24y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =6.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4-{e}^{6x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07dc3b58-eba0-5097-b69a-33c5acc27717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{9}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360865} {:answer/id #uuid "190d9a53-3469-5e7f-b03f-2cf38a49c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{9}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360936} {:answer/id #uuid "404a584d-5be0-5c62-874f-3e3012cc296c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361125} {:answer/id #uuid "46d918e7-6c1c-54ae-9afa-2a28c3b9aae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361143} {:answer/id #uuid "d39f0726-0e78-5f3d-a8ec-67d31d858e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361784} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bf37d9f-29ab-5f48-be79-4562eb844e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1695f8e-4929-5966-a2ac-c737d784a8b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-99006" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $-5{f}^{′}(x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\int \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81223159-c5d4-588d-9314-66a3a430542a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369184} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e66f76-6ad4-5bdc-abf7-bebed8056693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369185} {:answer/id #uuid "634bfffa-5e14-55f7-a686-f6fb8d6cec2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369186} {:answer/id #uuid "e31e6823-8cbb-53ae-bb81-764ac833481b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369187} {:answer/id #uuid "99c6a89a-45a4-5739-bf2f-f0c0f3684523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369185} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e6da79-a939-5e5c-a0d3-eb489cc25e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b02d1c4-7d7d-53ea-825a-e7c0df24295a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115108" :question/problem "![](images/93/93eeedaad213d620382392235ac91835106dd772892c8c3e4ec74e808ae22179.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a085c46-201d-5ed3-be42-2030b699b7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370001} :answer-field/id #uuid "2effdd59-bc2f-5ad0-a07a-23dc11d9d6e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b82726dc-a173-5a38-aefa-ebf1870acad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-277919" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9bb9fd502e37eb4e96c91c86a48833431b8f54c785f9dd096e8dc0842a2389f7.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7341349-5722-538a-a46b-c46b327387d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{225}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370004} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b13a75-2938-57e9-b343-79d1cfb33366" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d8eb6498-fe7d-54d8-8156-e7257b7cf3ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-279127" :question/problem "![](images/92/92752dfc375c895c72d3a053a9abfd64eb64ecf9660e24b0c731e7ae9616ae7b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{15}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{225}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{225}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f12915-dd40-5ab5-9c8d-d5b8239ddb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370007} :answer-field/id #uuid "f45ec21e-afa6-5535-91ea-60e2824f8700" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac9d8686-4f69-5072-a6be-b7169a3cf7dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-279141" :question/problem "![](images/24/244cfd754107b056748bd6049c2996f9c0a0ae990f2033005b2f96e5abf1c0ea.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=9.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34b4a4f-ec93-5b79-9836-24c30f1a306c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370010} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e92adb-417b-51d5-9a32-861203d3639b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4d8222d-c914-54e1-8111-88a395566578" :question/math-academy-id "q-279143" :question/problem "![](images/68/682eaeaa8b8ef428ab181953ab577e1fe91009996b83f0799a7684d8b9efb21d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{225}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{225} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e02416ae-0c05-5603-a201-57564dfca3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{289}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370013} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21d9fe8-5a2d-55ae-9ce6-47bd81a95ce0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c883bff-1508-545d-b07f-f784ee6e3f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-279145" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e5123d9581d8b9ee1cc32358519c505c47f6e976a2fa28ff1939c769bebcdc9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{17}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{289}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{289} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983ea1d4-591d-51d0-91a0-baacaa311a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370016} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcb5f98-9095-45e6-b3f5-ea343bdb888d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x^2}{25}+\\frac{4y^2}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370016} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0c2ace-3d6d-5114-b445-c9cf46779b74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb865dca-23c4-59c4-bd03-8c9d8005b669" :question/math-academy-id "q-279154" :question/problem "![](images/92/9291f02901a4a05150dfadd309cc7508209c0f249d9b07a8185d1259f67d7296.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{5}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1f7bc7-0cdd-5b27-8fe9-02b806603eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370019} :answer-field/id #uuid "357e7c5e-4b53-50fa-852d-a2a0c73a078a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11b50d3f-8a2a-53bd-9460-027070f70e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-279155" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9deebb67265aafd4fd2c3e7451dddc837c928242ac016a0632907816defcdec1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{11}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{121}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{121} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b4d7d8-6b13-51b3-8c16-f1f796b0790a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022} :answer-field/id #uuid "1784709b-8c12-545a-96ba-dc167b20f7c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70a177c-e321-5d97-ad0f-2390d9f8f894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c229291-398d-5b8b-8d76-841b2f6ef86c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a5d8fed-b754-51cf-933f-f6f47cc1ecb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279198" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=8$ and ${b}^{2}=24.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=8,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=24,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{24}=2\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{6},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{6}=4\\sqrt{6},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65eb186c-e14b-5e5b-b246-910f532877ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370995} {:answer/id #uuid "ba12a02d-2a23-5848-a775-1a9517753135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370996} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8fdc06-cbcf-5c84-9ddf-a56eb7032043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370997} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeee171-e500-5146-8d03-5a5c1bd03a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370998} {:answer/id #uuid "4041e09f-b99a-5bc3-af3a-1a97f34bb588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370998} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ed664f-5269-578c-9dc2-0e5ea600cf72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bf44446-fd59-5eea-85f3-78a8c0b563be" :question/math-academy-id "q-69890" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a7f7fc248f5582afea5240aa2f7c5f6ee421a53961640efbfae18b9c920c10d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158f36f0-5c9f-5df4-b307-3df00e1927ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\pi +2-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371854} {:answer/id #uuid "394931cf-7701-52c5-939f-4d7925927a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371855} {:answer/id #uuid "8a89762a-446c-538c-a5f1-db6c984332b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\pi +1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371856} {:answer/id #uuid "20d10a3f-7efd-565e-8b3b-25e4aed18fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\pi -4+4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371857} {:answer/id #uuid "38fc6ca0-2882-53b5-a94f-8c1db20782e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{\\pi -1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371855} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6da82b-caa9-5a2a-8f75-9c0f26f5be8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a14aaf63-21a2-55cb-ab96-06f1083b96bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-14309" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\n$$  that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C}.\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)+C} \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{4}+C} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1}=\\frac{4}{\\pi +4-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f219f76-9737-5821-a859-30aa164ad6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372693} {:answer/id #uuid "2d435dfc-8fb9-5fc0-a344-d7022166af19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2}(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372694} {:answer/id #uuid "57a48c5c-6c1b-59ea-991b-8f7a3b9cac74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372695} {:answer/id #uuid "8899c662-4eda-56dd-9309-028ca91e5cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k(90+V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372696} {:answer/id #uuid "8a58c995-7408-580c-986d-705cb9bbdb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372697} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc91c9af-72e4-5170-a068-194980f1aace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0b55ff2-bf59-5ae2-ab99-23109ff31f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-335137" :question/problem "A shipping company’s management noted that the growth of a ship’s speed is directly proportional to the difference between the ship’s maximum cruising speed and its current speed. Let $V(t)$ be the speed of the ship, measured in kilometers per hour, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in hours. If the maximum cruising speed of the ship is $90$ $\\text{km/h}$, which differential equation could be used to model the ship’s speed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the speed $V(t)$ of the ship changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum cruising speed and the current speed. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-V),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum cruising speed of the ship, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=90\\text{ km/h},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(90-V).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cad08fd0-a60c-5289-bfae-917d24b4cf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-x-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373005} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01b75b0-e7f0-5237-8118-7d26642ee499" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1491d28-f486-5d5a-8bd7-310f4893ab88" :question/math-academy-id "q-355560" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies $y(2)=-1.$\n\n|  | $y(x)=$ | $x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =(4{x}^{-2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (4{x}^{-2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -{y}^{-1} & =-4{x}^{-1}+x+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{4}{x}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x}-x-C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{4-{x}^{2}-Cx}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{4-{x}^{2}-Cx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(2)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4-{2}^{2}-C(2)} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{-2C} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =-1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{x}{4-x-{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}]}
